  },
  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.3 × loss. Steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first: the x direction zig-zags above learning rate 0.25 and grows from step to step above 0.5.",
    "Noise is Gaussian around the true slope with spread 3·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. A single step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows show steps, not gradients: learning rate × slope estimate, pointing the way the ball moves, drawn at 2× true length. Mini-batch step uses the noisy estimate, Full-data step uses the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. A path point off the bowl is drawn at its rim.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence. Reset replays it from the start. A new run uses the next values. Trails hold the start point plus up to 40 steps."
  ]
}